{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And you shall make, etc., for honor and for splendor.</b> One needs to know what is the intent of HaShem in saying <b>for honor</b>, etc., after He has already informed us of their making and their form; and if it is to inform us that these garments bring Aaron honor and splendor, what do we gain from this? Perhaps it may be explained based on what they said in the chapter Ein Ma'amidin (Avodah Zarah 34a), and this is their language: They asked Rabbi Akiva — with what did Moses serve during the seven days of ordination? He did not have it [i.e., did not know]. He came and asked in the study hall. They said to him: with a white tunic that has no hem, end of quote. From the fact that Moses served during the seven days of ordination in this tunic, it indicates that the garments are a prerequisite only for Aaron and his sons, but for someone other than the priests, the sacrificial service would be valid without the garments. And this is what He informs him by saying <b>for honor</b>, etc. — to say: do not think that the garments are for the needs of the service alone, such that the matter would require Moses to wear them during the service of the days of ordination; but it is not so. They are rather <b>for honor</b>, etc. — for the office appointed to his service; but as for the service itself, when it is performed by someone other than Aaron and his sons, they may serve in a white tunic, just as Moses indeed served as mentioned." "<b>Or</b> it may say that HaShem comes to give a reason why He commanded eight garments — four white and four of gold — and He says the reason is <b>for honor and for splendor.</b> That is, according to their words, may their memory be a blessing (introduction to Tikunei Zohar), the eight garments — the four white garments allude to the four letters of the name YHWH, blessed be He, and the four golden garments allude to the four letters of the name Adonai. And know that the name YHWH, blessed be He, is associated with the epithet of Tiferet, as is known, and the name Adonai is associated with the epithet of honor (kavod). And that is what it says: <b>And you shall make garments of holiness</b>, etc., <b>for honor</b> — corresponding to the golden garments; <b>and for splendor</b> — corresponding to the white garments. And the verse arranged the order of the gradations of holiness, one above the other, and placed <b>for honor</b> first — and understand." "And HaShem commanded making eight garments, through which would be atoned the blemishes that human beings cause when they reach the supernal place, and through this that sin would be atoned. And along this path one may give a good reason why HaShem was insistent about the garments for Aaron but not for Moses, according to their saying, may their memory be a blessing (Arachin 16a), that the eight priestly garments atone for eight kinds of transgressions that may occur among the children of Israel — therefore HaShem commanded the matter only for the priest who stands to minister, he and his sons, all the days, to atone for the children of Israel. This is not so for Moses, for all that he served during the seven days of ordination was only for the atonement of Aaron and his sons, to fill their hands to serve before HaShem — there was no need for the garments to atone as mentioned; therefore he served in a white tunic, etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:34:05.380192269Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}